      en: 'Standing on a raised mound in Bibichini village, Betagi upazila, Barguna, the single-domed Bibichini Shahi Mosque is one of the oldest Muslim monuments in the region. Tradition holds that it was built in the seventeenth century, in the time of Mughal governor Shah Shuja, by a saint named Shah Nimatullah, and that the village is named after his daughter, Chini Bibi.\n\nThough small, the mosque has a dignified presence: thick walls, a single large dome and Mughal-style arches. From the mound you look out over green village fields, and nearby are a few old graves that local people believe belong to the saint’s family.\n\nSome 350 years on, prayers are still held here regularly. The mosque is protected as an archaeological heritage site and is a peaceful, rewarding detour for anyone interested in Mughal-era Bengal.',
